<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Diaspora.Modules.CommunityProgramme</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Diaspora.Modules.CommunityProgramme</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Community Programme Platform (Phase B18). What an organization runs for the people who
      belong to it — a membership programme with tiers, a volunteer programme with shifts and
      confirmed hours, polls with server-computed results, scoped announcements, and the impact and
      analytics reads over all four.

      This is not a second Community Life, and the boundary is the module's central claim. B12 owns
      the organization, the event, the RSVP and — this is the one most easily got wrong — the
      (member, organization, role) edge itself. B18 owns nothing about who belongs to a body; it
      owns what a body offers them. Every authorization question this module asks is answered by
      B12's published IOrganizationDirectory, and the ten-flag OrganizationCapabilities contract is
      a projection of B12's role ladder, not a second one.

      Nor is it a second anything else. Money is B15's: a tier carries a price expressed in a
      currency B15's catalogue validates, and not one penny moves through this module. Delivery is
      B13's: this module publishes integration events and never composes a notification. Ranking is
      B14's. Scheduling is B17's seam. Recurrence is B12.1's. The fundraising campaign lifecycle
      belongs to B15.1 and is deliberately absent here — amended 11 August 2026: B15.1 shipped it,
      and this module now maps its routes without owning any of it. /communities/campaigns/... and
      /communities/memberships/{id}/payments are contributed here because the platform allows one
      module per URL prefix and this module owns /api/v1/communities; every aggregate, state machine
      and figure behind them is B15's, reached through injected B15 services. The same split this
      module already uses for the one Community Life write it exposes: the route is ours and the
      decision is theirs.

      What B18 owns: the membership programme and its tiers, the fourteen-state programme
      membership, volunteer opportunities, applications, assignments, confirmed hours and
      certificates, community polls and their responses, community announcements, and the read
      surfaces — organization analytics, community impact and the federated Community Life search —
      computed over those rows and over what B12 and B15 already publish.

      Idempotency is B15's primitive, copied rather than reinvented: a nullable key column on the
      aggregate the write creates, uniquely indexed, looked up before the write and replayed on a
      repeat. There is no second idempotency store.

      Registers entirely through B2.6 contributor interfaces: no edit to the Platform Kernel, to
      Identity, or to Program.cs.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Two module references, both deliberate and both read-only in intent.

    Community Life, because B18 is an extension of it. The organization, its membership edge and
    its role ladder are B12's, and this module reaches all three through the published
    IOrganizationDirectory contract rather than through B12's aggregates or repositories. The one
    write B18 exposes on B12's behalf — assigning an organization role — is dispatched as B12's own
    command through MediatR, so B12's handler makes the decision and B12 stays the only writer of
    a role.

    Financial, for one thing only: the currency catalogue. A tier price is Money, and the register
    is explicit that there is to be no second currency source. B18 validates a currency code
    against B15's catalogue and stores an amount; it creates no payment, no donation, no
    subscription and no wallet movement, and an architecture test asserts it.

    Notably absent: Notifications and Recommendations. This module publishes integration events and
    B13 consumes them; a reference in the other direction would be the first cycle in the graph
    B2.6 exists to keep acyclic. Location, Media, Housing and the sibling verticals are absent for
    the same reason B12 gives — a place is reached through B12's summary, not through B4.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.SharedKernel\Diaspora.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Contracts\Diaspora.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Platform\Diaspora.Platform.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Application\Diaspora.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Persistence\Diaspora.Persistence.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Modules.CommunityLife\Diaspora.Modules.CommunityLife.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Modules.Financial\Diaspora.Modules.Financial.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
